WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:01.417
Travel Visa.

2
00:00:01.697 --> 00:00:06.198
Day 27 of the Soft Skill Playbook course, in the Everyday Life phase.

3
00:00:07.948 --> 00:00:08.972
The key idea.

4
00:00:09.252 --> 00:00:19.087
Today's goal: understand visa types, ask the right questions about documents and timelines, and answer visa-interview questions clearly, briefly and honestly.

5
00:00:19.367 --> 00:00:21.585
You'll need all of this before you book the flight.

6
00:00:22.935 --> 00:00:23.703
Info:

7
00:00:23.983 --> 00:00:25.519
Rules differ by country.

8
00:00:25.799 --> 00:00:33.266
Visa types, documents, fees and processing times change often and depend on the country you're visiting and your passport.

9
00:00:33.546 --> 00:00:40.479
Always check the official embassy or consulate website, or the official visa application centre, before you apply.

10
00:00:40.759 --> 00:00:42.807
This page is about communication.

11
00:00:44.157 --> 00:00:45.011
Part 1.

12
00:00:45.291 --> 00:00:46.251
Key words.

13
00:00:47.501 --> 00:00:48.866
Here is a quick table:

14
00:00:49.146 --> 00:01:09.562
Word or phrase, and Meaning. tourist or business or work or student or transit visa: visa types. single-entry or multiple-entry: enter once or many times. validity or duration of stay: how long the visa is valid or how long you may stay each visit. embassy or consulate or visa application centre

15
00:01:09.842 --> 00:01:22.023
(VAC): where visas are handled. appointment or biometrics: booked slot or fingerprints and photo. invitation letter or sponsor: a letter from the host or person or company paying.

16
00:01:23.373 --> 00:01:43.405
itinerary or travel insurance or hotel booking: travel plan documents. bank statements or ITR or salary slips: proof of funds and income. no-objection certificate (NOC) / leave letter: letter from your employer. approved or refused or administrative processing: visa results.

17
00:01:44.755 --> 00:01:45.630
Part 2.

18
00:01:45.910 --> 00:01:47.126
Questions you'll ask.

19
00:01:48.376 --> 00:01:49.443
Before applying.

20
00:01:49.723 --> 00:01:52.816
Which visa type do I need for a two-day client meeting?

21
00:01:53.096 --> 00:01:54.675
What documents do I need?

22
00:01:54.955 --> 00:01:56.149
Is there a checklist?

23
00:01:56.429 --> 00:01:58.157
How long does processing take?

24
00:01:58.437 --> 00:02:00.571
Can I apply for priority processing?

25
00:02:00.851 --> 00:02:03.325
How early can I apply before my travel date?

26
00:02:03.605 --> 00:02:05.888
Do I need an appointment for biometrics?

27
00:02:06.168 --> 00:02:07.789
Do I need travel insurance?

28
00:02:08.069 --> 00:02:09.435
What's the minimum cover?

29
00:02:09.715 --> 00:02:11.955
How much is the fee, and how do I pay?

30
00:02:13.305 --> 00:02:14.521
At the visa centre.

31
00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:16.593
Where do I submit my documents?

32
00:02:16.873 --> 00:02:19.283
Do you need the originals or only copies?

33
00:02:19.563 --> 00:02:22.422
Can I keep my passport while the visa is processed?

34
00:02:22.702 --> 00:02:25.390
How will I know when the passport is ready to collect?

35
00:02:25.670 --> 00:02:27.590
Can someone else collect my passport?

36
00:02:28.940 --> 00:02:29.836
Part 3.

37
00:02:30.116 --> 00:02:32.548
Visa interview: questions you'll be asked.

38
00:02:33.798 --> 00:02:39.345
Golden rule: answer exactly what's asked, briefly and honestly, and have documents ready.

39
00:02:40.695 --> 00:02:42.060
Here is a quick table:

40
00:02:42.340 --> 00:02:44.303
Officer asks, and Good answer.

41
00:02:44.583 --> 00:02:46.076
What's the purpose of your trip?:

42
00:02:46.356 --> 00:02:50.559
I'm attending a three-day product workshop at our client's office in Berlin.

43
00:02:50.839 --> 00:02:52.289
Who is paying for the trip?:

44
00:02:52.569 --> 00:02:53.935
My company is paying.

45
00:02:54.215 --> 00:02:57.201
Here's the invitation and the letter from my employer.

46
00:02:57.481 --> 00:02:58.847
How long will you stay?:

47
00:02:59.127 --> 00:03:01.857
Five days, from the 10th to the 15th of March.

48
00:03:02.137 --> 00:03:03.289
Where will you stay?:

49
00:03:03.569 --> 00:03:06.151
At the Hotel Adlon, near the client's office.

50
00:03:06.431 --> 00:03:07.540
Here's the booking.

51
00:03:07.820 --> 00:03:08.823
What do you do?:

52
00:03:09.103 --> 00:03:13.647
I'm a senior software engineer at XYZ Technologies in Bengaluru.

53
00:03:13.927 --> 00:03:15.505
I've been there for six years.

54
00:03:15.785 --> 00:03:17.535
Why should we believe you'll return?:

55
00:03:17.815 --> 00:03:23.745
I have a permanent job, my family lives in Bengaluru, and I have a return ticket for the 15th.

56
00:03:25.095 --> 00:03:26.887
Have you travelled abroad before?:

57
00:03:27.167 --> 00:03:33.354
Yes, to Singapore in 2023 and to Dubai in 2024, both for work.

58
00:03:33.634 --> 00:03:35.170
Do you have relatives there?:

59
00:03:35.450 --> 00:03:40.314
No, I don't. / Yes, my cousin lives in Munich, but I'm staying at the hotel.

60
00:03:41.664 --> 00:03:42.411
Warning:

61
00:03:42.691 --> 00:03:43.352
Don't.

62
00:03:43.632 --> 00:03:51.141
Memorise long speeches, give extra information that wasn't asked for, guess dates or numbers, say anything you can't prove.

63
00:03:51.421 --> 00:03:52.893
If you don't understand, say:

64
00:03:53.173 --> 00:03:55.093
Sorry, could you repeat the question?

65
00:03:56.443 --> 00:03:57.361
Part 4.

66
00:03:57.641 --> 00:04:00.777
Conversation 1: at the visa application centre.

67
00:04:02.027 --> 00:04:04.053
Here is how the conversation goes.

68
00:04:04.333 --> 00:04:05.741
Good morning.

69
00:04:06.021 --> 00:04:08.112
Appointment letter and passport, please.

70
00:04:08.392 --> 00:04:09.480
Good morning.

71
00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:10.912
Here you are.

72
00:04:11.192 --> 00:04:13.965
I'm applying for a business visa for Germany.

73
00:04:14.245 --> 00:04:16.080
Do you have the document checklist?

74
00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.064
Yes.

75
00:04:17.344 --> 00:04:21.440
Everything is in this folder, in the same order as the checklist.

76
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:22.829
Thank you.

77
00:04:23.109 --> 00:04:25.136
The invitation letter is a copy.

78
00:04:25.416 --> 00:04:26.803
Do you have the original?

79
00:04:28.153 --> 00:04:29.753
The conversation continues.

80
00:04:30.033 --> 00:04:31.825
The client sent it by email.

81
00:04:32.105 --> 00:04:34.174
Is a printed copy acceptable?

82
00:04:34.454 --> 00:04:36.011
For this category, yes.

83
00:04:36.291 --> 00:04:37.443
Please sign here.

84
00:04:37.723 --> 00:04:40.177
Next, go to counter 4 for biometrics.

85
00:04:40.457 --> 00:04:41.395
Okay.

86
00:04:41.675 --> 00:04:44.022
How long does processing usually take?

87
00:04:44.302 --> 00:04:46.115
About 15 working days.

88
00:04:46.395 --> 00:04:48.849
You'll get an SMS when the passport is ready.

89
00:04:50.199 --> 00:04:51.799
The conversation continues.

90
00:04:52.079 --> 00:04:54.105
Can I track the status online?

91
00:04:54.385 --> 00:04:56.775
Yes, with the reference number on this receipt.

92
00:04:57.055 --> 00:04:58.420
Thank you very much.

93
00:04:59.770 --> 00:05:00.730
Part 5.

94
00:05:01.010 --> 00:05:03.271
Conversation 2: the visa interview.

95
00:05:04.521 --> 00:05:06.548
Here is how the conversation goes.

96
00:05:06.828 --> 00:05:08.236
Good morning.

97
00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:10.991
What's the purpose of your trip to the United States?

98
00:05:11.271 --> 00:05:12.359
Good morning.

99
00:05:12.639 --> 00:05:16.223
I'm attending a five-day technical conference in Seattle.

100
00:05:16.503 --> 00:05:18.017
Who is paying for the trip?

101
00:05:18.297 --> 00:05:19.407
My company.

102
00:05:19.687 --> 00:05:21.607
Here's the letter from my employer.

103
00:05:21.887 --> 00:05:23.529
How long have you worked there?

104
00:05:24.879 --> 00:05:26.479
The conversation continues.

105
00:05:26.759 --> 00:05:27.847
Six years.

106
00:05:28.127 --> 00:05:30.773
I'm a senior engineer on the payments team.

107
00:05:31.053 --> 00:05:32.141
Are you married?

108
00:05:32.421 --> 00:05:33.125
Yes.

109
00:05:33.405 --> 00:05:36.711
My wife and my son live with me in Bengaluru.

110
00:05:36.991 --> 00:05:38.847
Have you been to the US before?

111
00:05:39.127 --> 00:05:40.941
No, this is my first time.

112
00:05:41.221 --> 00:05:44.335
I've travelled to Singapore and Dubai for work.

113
00:05:45.685 --> 00:05:47.285
The conversation continues.

114
00:05:47.565 --> 00:05:48.995
When are you coming back?

115
00:05:49.275 --> 00:05:51.152
On the 22nd of June.

116
00:05:51.432 --> 00:05:53.245
My return ticket is booked.

117
00:05:53.525 --> 00:05:54.635
Okay.

118
00:05:54.915 --> 00:05:56.536
Your visa is approved.

119
00:05:56.816 --> 00:05:58.885
You'll get your passport back by courier.

120
00:05:59.165 --> 00:06:00.083
Thank you!

121
00:06:01.433 --> 00:06:02.371
Part 6.

122
00:06:02.651 --> 00:06:04.102
Follow-up and problems.

123
00:06:05.352 --> 00:06:08.957
My application has been in processing for 25 days.

124
00:06:09.237 --> 00:06:11.179
Could you tell me if anything is missing?

125
00:06:11.459 --> 00:06:13.315
My travel date is next week.

126
00:06:13.595 --> 00:06:16.048
Is it possible to expedite the application?

127
00:06:16.328 --> 00:06:17.843
My visa was refused.

128
00:06:18.123 --> 00:06:20.640
Could you tell me the reason, and can I reapply?

129
00:06:20.920 --> 00:06:23.480
There's a spelling mistake in my name on the visa.

130
00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:25.189
How do I get it corrected?

131
00:06:26.539 --> 00:06:27.478
Part 7.

132
00:06:27.758 --> 00:06:28.675
Practice.

133
00:06:29.925 --> 00:06:37.371
Answer these in one sentence each, for a trip you'd like to take: purpose, length of stay, who pays, your job, why you'll return.

134
00:06:38.721 --> 00:06:39.638
Success:

135
00:06:39.918 --> 00:06:41.070
Sample answers.

136
00:06:41.350 --> 00:06:44.102
I'm visiting Japan on holiday with my family.

137
00:06:44.382 --> 00:06:47.198
Ten days, from the 3rd to the 13th of April.

138
00:06:47.478 --> 00:06:50.507
I am, from my savings; here are my bank statements.

139
00:06:50.787 --> 00:06:53.881
I'm a software engineer at XYZ Technologies.

140
00:06:54.161 --> 00:06:59.025
I have a permanent job and my parents live with me here; my return ticket is booked.

141
00:07:00.375 --> 00:07:01.249
Part 8.

142
00:07:01.529 --> 00:07:02.575
Speak today.

143
00:07:03.825 --> 00:07:08.646
Practise the interview table out loud until each answer is under 15 seconds.

144
00:07:08.926 --> 00:07:11.785
Make a document checklist in English for your next trip.

145
00:07:13.135 --> 00:07:14.031
Part 9.

146
00:07:14.311 --> 00:07:17.767
Practice conversation: asking HR for a visa letter.

147
00:07:19.017 --> 00:07:21.043
Here is how the conversation goes.

148
00:07:21.323 --> 00:07:25.910
Hi Sunita, I need an employer letter for my German business visa.

149
00:07:26.190 --> 00:07:26.894
Sure.

150
00:07:27.174 --> 00:07:28.561
What should it include?

151
00:07:28.841 --> 00:07:35.561
My job title, how long I've worked here, my salary, the purpose of the trip, and that the company is paying.

152
00:07:35.841 --> 00:07:36.886
And the dates?

153
00:07:38.236 --> 00:07:39.836
The conversation continues.

154
00:07:40.116 --> 00:07:42.911
Travel from the 10th to the 15th of March.

155
00:07:43.191 --> 00:07:46.753
The embassy also needs it on company letterhead, signed.

156
00:07:47.033 --> 00:07:48.143
Okay.

157
00:07:48.423 --> 00:07:50.172
I'll have it ready by Thursday.

158
00:07:50.452 --> 00:07:51.369
Thank you!

159
00:07:51.649 --> 00:07:54.444
Could you also send me a scanned copy by email?

160
00:07:55.794 --> 00:07:57.330
That's the end of this lesson.

161
00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:02.282
Pick one script from it, say it out loud three times, and use it for real today.

162
00:08:02.562 --> 00:08:08.663
For extra practice, paste the Master Prompt into an AI assistant and role-play the conversation.

